<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>@EXECUTABLE@</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>@BUNDLE_ID@</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>@NAME@</string>
    <!-- THE DECLARED APP IDENTITY, or nothing at all
         (docs/app-identity-plan.md ruling 3). iOS has NO runtime route to
         the Home Screen icon — the SDK's whole app-icon surface is
         supportsAlternateIcons/setAlternateIconName/alternateIconName,
         typed BOOL and NSString, none of which takes bytes — so this
         bundle is where an iOS app's identity lives, and make_bundle
         fills these keys from guests/assets/identity.toml.

         IT IS PER-BUNDLE AND NOT GLOBAL, deliberately: a guest that
         declares no identity must produce a bundle carrying none, or the
         interpreter's bundle read could report an icon for an app that
         never declared one. That is the vacuous pass this whole scene
         exists to make impossible.

         CFBundleIconFiles NAMES A FILE IN THE BUNDLE ROOT, which is the
         pre-asset-catalog spelling and the only one available here: there
         is no asset catalog in this tree and no `actool` in the dev
         shell, and the declared mark is copied in VERBATIM because
         tools/check-app-identity.py C5 holds every app-icon resource in
         the tree byte-identical to the declaration. -->
    @IDENTITY@
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.0.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- 1 = iPhone, 2 = iPad. Absent, this defaults to iPhone ONLY,
         and iPadOS then runs the bundle in iPhone COMPATIBILITY mode:
         a phone-sized window scaled up on an iPad, reporting a compact
         horizontal size class. That silently made the iPad leg a
         second phone leg — the device was a real iPad Pro while the
         app was not an iPad app at all. Declaring both is what makes
         a regular size class reachable. -->
    <key>UIDeviceFamily</key>
    <array>
        <integer>1</integer>
        <integer>2</integer>
    </array>
    <!-- WITHOUT THESE TWO THE PICKER CANNOT SEE THIS APP'S FILES.
         The document picker browses document PROVIDERS, and an app's
         container is not one of them until it says so: UIFileSharingEnabled
         publishes Documents/, and LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace makes it
         browsable in place rather than through a copy. The filedialog scene
         writes its two files there and aims the picker at them, so without
         the keys the picker opens somewhere else entirely — silently, which
         is the failure shape this whole milestone keeps meeting. Measured
         with tools/ios/pickerprobe. -->
    <key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- PORTRAIT, PINNED, ON EVERY LEG. Absent this key an app inherits
         the DEVICE's orientation, and a leg's window width is then
         unpinned state nobody set: measured 2026-08-29, the same phone
         reports 375x734 in portrait and 724x355 in landscape, and
         `adaptive`'s breakpoint is declared at 520 — so `expect_axis
         row@narrow "vertical"` is TRUE in portrait and FALSE in
         landscape, on the same build, with no code between them. The leg
         failed intermittently for exactly that sentence
         (docs/deferred.md's ios-flaky entry) and no rerun could say why,
         because the verdict was correct both times and only the premise
         moved. The pool's device TYPE is pinned a few lines up in
         run-sim.py for the same reason; its geometry was not.
         BOTH FAMILIES, deliberately: the pad leg reads a REGULAR width
         and portrait iPad (1032) is regular, so one rule serves both. -->
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    </array>
    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict/>
</dict>
</plist>
